ide what Hawaii was going  to be and So to avoid accusations of  Empire President Eisenhower offered the  territory a vote on becoming a state  over 90 percent of Hawaiians supported  the move and so in 1959 it joined as  America's 50th state so why didn't  Puerto Rico get the same treatment the  United Nations also took a dim view of  America keeping it as a territory but  the difference was in the attitudes of  the Puerto Ricans to the United States  from before its annexation until the  outbreak of World War One the island had  a strong independence movement and very  few people there wanted to become  Americans yet after Puerto Ricans were  given citizenship in 1917 the desire for  Independence slowly faded and people  came to prefer release association with  the United States this didn't mean that  everybody was happy with the arrangement  and when the island status was reviewed  in the 1950s small-scale armed revolts  broke out across the island despite this  most Puerto Ricans were happy to remain  a U.S territory they didn't get any seat  in Congress but no representation meant  no taxation and so those who lived there  didn't have to pay federal income tax as  for the opinion of successive U.S  governments they didn't want to deal  with the potential upheaval that extra  senators and congressmen would bring and  during the 1950s and 1960s when certain  political and social issues with the  Forefront of American thought there were  concerns about emitting another state  whose population was overwhelmingly  non-white and whose population was about  three times larger than both Alaska and  Hawaii's combined as such in the  American political tradition it was time  for can kicking something which  continues to this day is opinions on the  island slowly move towards being more  and more pro-stated I hope you enjoyed  this episode and a special thanks to my  patrons James bisonet Kelly moneymaker  Sky Chappelle kosher wolf Jerry lambden  Jordan Longley Adam Stalt